Thanks for your considered response, Patric. You make some very
reasonable points throughout your post. I especially like the quote
above - you are right, for Nethack to have grown into its current state
while preserving balance is a laudable achievement, and maybe the
'higher turnover' process I am envisaging would endanger that - or at
least, there would have to be careful consideration as to how balance
issues would be kept in check.


> As a fellow software engineer I have a question to you. Have you
> already looked at the source code of NetHack? Looking at it is a
> little like using a time machine to go back to the 80s.

I have seen it a little, just while reconciling overlapping patches
that wouldn't play nice together out of the box. Yes, the style of C
used has its own flavour, presumably by historical necessity which is
now maintained for reasons which are not obvious to a casual inspector
of the code, and for stylistic consistancy. Beyond that though, the
code's pedigree is indicated even more clearly by the fact that it's
written in C at all - if you're coding because you love to code, then C
is a perfect choice, if you catch my drift (:



